{
  "case_id": "2010-10-6",
  "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:13 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The standard for the state of mind element for a claim for inducing patent infringement should be: Did the accused inducer have a purpose to induce a third party to engage in acts that the accused inducer knew infringed the patent? That's what I'll call the purposeful, culpable test."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument next this morning in Case 10-6, Global-Tech Appliances v. SEB S.A."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Willful blindness would not fall within our purposeful, culpable test."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: You said that the particular patent -- the defendant would have to know that the device infringed the particular patent. I think that would be a standard that would be impossible to meet."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: No, (b) and (c) have different standards as to -- not the knowledge of the patent but -- but what the person is -- the acts that constitute infringement. But that's a different thing from whether they should have different standards as to the knowledge of the patent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I'm -- I'm a little confused. If you knew that there was a patent under (b), and you still gave the fryer -- patented the fryer that you know it is a patented fryer to Montgomery Ward or Sunbeam to sell, you're not liable under (b), because why? What act of yours was not purposeful?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: I take it that we would do that on the basis of Aro II? We would just say that's the standard for (c) and that should be the statement for (b)?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Wait. Before you go -\u00ad before you go further, what if you do that, okay?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cruz",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CRUZ: If they had said three words differently, this would be a very, very different case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Why aren't you both wrong, that Grokster didn't deal with the question that we are dealing with, which was knowledge of a patent or knowledge of a copyright, that Grokster dealt with whether there was specific intent or whether there needed to be specific intent to encourage infringing acts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Well, one of the -- one of the briefs -- one of the amicus briefs in this case points out that that argument is a lot less plausible in copyright than it is in -- in patents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the district court observed below this case was not a close case. It took the jury 109 minutes to resolve against Pentalpha on every single ground that was presented to it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dunnegan",
      "case_id": "2010-10-6",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8bf6088c85e002e83dc688854e97ccc6eb234dca185196d00bbf2740b7d91ed2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8bf6088c85e0-0002-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DUNNEGAN: Five points in rebuttal, I believe, Your Honor. The first is what should the standard be with respect to willful blindness."
    },
    {
